## A B S T R A C T
Polysaccharide/MOF composite membranes have captured the interests of many researchers during decontam­ ination of polluted environments. Their popularity can be attributed to the relatively high chemical and thermal stabilities of these composite membranes. Chitosan is among the polysaccharides extensively used during the synthesis of hybrid membranes with MOFs. The applications of chitosan/MOF composite membranes in sepa­ ration science are explored in detail in this paper. Researchers have also synthesised mixed matrix membranes of MOFs with cellulose and cyclodextrin that have proved to be effective during separation of a variety of materials. The uses of cellulose/MOF and cyclodextrin/MOF membranes for the removal of environmental pollutants are discussed in this review. In addition, the challenges associated with the use of these mixed matrix membranes are explored in this current paper.
## 1. Introduction
Human activities have resulted in the release of many pollutants into the environment with harmful effects to flora and fauna (Duong et al., 2021; Robinson et al., 2021; Wang, Ito, et al., 2021). These pollutants include agro-chemicals, pharmaceuticals, radioactive waste and green­ house gases such as $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and oxides of nitrogen. Some agrochemicals, for instance, are endocrine disruptors (Draskau et al., 2021; Paul et al., 2021) and others cause the malfunctioning of the repro­ ductive (Fucic et al., 2021; Ledda et al., 2021), nervous (Dassanayake et al., 2021; Ghasemnejad-Berenji et al., 2021) and cardiovascular sys­ tems (Encarnacao et al., 2019; Shiyovich et al., 2018) in living organ­ isms. Greenhouse gases, on the other hand, cause excessive increase in global temperatures and this may result in climate change and some serious health issues (Massar et al., 2021; Mona et al., 2021; Parthiban et al., 2021). Thus, concerted efforts should be directed towards finding effective methods to decontaminate the environment and avert their devastating effects on flora and fauna.
Many techniques have been used by researchers during environ­ mental remediation and in separation science. These include phytor­ emediation (Brunhoferova et al., 2021; El Awady et al., 2021), biosorption (Ghaffariraad & Lak, 2021; Kamyabi et al., 2021) and adsorp­ tion (El Khomri et al., 2022; Ngofa et al., 2022). Phytoremediation and bio-sorption are environmentally friendly and relatively cheap techniques. They are, however, very slow remediation processes. Adsorption, on the other hand, is very efficient but suffers from limita­ tions such as high operational costs and energy-consuming adsorbent regeneration process. An alternative technique, which has captured the interests of many researchers, is membrane technology (Klepic et al., 2021; Lavanya et al., 2021; Singh & Saroj, 2021; Soyekwo et al., 2021). Membrane separation is an energy efficient and eco-friendly process that can be applied during the removal of many pollutants from contami­ nated environmental samples (Carter et al., 2021; Mishra et al., 2021; Pang et al., 2021) (Fig. 1A). The important factors that affect membrane performance include porosity, thermal resistance, swelling, gas per­ meance and selectivity, and these factors should be optimised. Poly­ meric and inorganic membranes have been used by researchers for the separation of chemicals from environmental samples (Asif & Zhang, 2021; Lopez et al., 2021).
Synthetic polymeric membranes such as polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) (Kamarudin et al., 2021; Kusuma et al., 2021), polyacrylonitrile (PAN) (Khaki et al., 2021; Shen et al., 2021), polyethersulfone (PES) (Johari et al., 2021; Samari et al., 2021) and polyethylene (PE) (da Silva et al., 2021; Li et al., 2021) have gained popularity among many re­ searchers due to their excellent chemical and mechanical strength. These synthetic polymeric membranes, however, are less appealing from an environmental point of view since they are non-biodegradable and relatively expensive. Consequently, natural polymeric membrane materials such as polysaccharides are slowly gaining popularity as they are relatively cheap and easily available (Bose et al., 2021; Lakkaboyana et al., 2021; Nechifor et al., 2021; Ounifi et al., 2021). Polysaccharides such as cyclodextrin (Gaalova et al., 2021; Topuz et al., 2021) (Fig. 1B), chitosan (Liu, Graham, and Yu, 2021; Tang et al., 2021) (Fig. 1C), cellulose (Behrouz et al., 2021; Mubashir et al., 2021) (Fig. 1D), and alginate (Khan et al., 2021; Zhang, Zhao, et al., 2021) (Fig. 1E) have been used to synthesis mixed matrix membranes by researchers. Mem­ branes that involve the use of pristine polysaccharides are less common as most of them lack mechanical strength and have relatively poor selectivity. Polysaccharide-based membranes have been modified in order to ensure high permeation and good selectivity during specific separation processes using techniques such as co-polymerisation, poly­ mer blending and cross-linking in order to enhance their physico­ chemical properties prior to their use during membrane separation. Thus, some researchers have stabilised polysaccharide-based mem­ branes through cross-linking and the formation of nanocomposites with materials such as graphene oxide (Amiri et al., 2021), poly (ether sul­ phone) (Hosseini et al., 2021), polycaprolactone nanofibers (Doan et al., 2021), polyvinylidene fluoride (Zhao et al., 2021) and metal organic frameworks (MOFs) (Cui et al., 2020).
Mixed matrix membranes made from polysaccharides and MOFs are popular in separation science (Khajavian et al., 2020; Penkova et al., 2021; Wu et al., 2017). Their popularity can be attributed to the attractive physico-chemical properties of MOFs. MOFs are coordination polymers, which consist of metal ion centres or metal clusters and organic ligands. They are highly porous and crystalline substances with remarkable chemical and mechanical strength (Cui et al., 2020; Schel­ ling et al., 2020). The common MOFs that are used in mixed matrix membranes include MIL (Materials Institute Lavoisier) (Mubashir et al., 2018; Su et al., 2015), UiO (University of Oslo) (Cui et al., 2020; Pish­ namazi et al., 2020), ZIF (Zeolitic Imidazolate Frameworks) (Pan et al., 2021; You et al., 2021)and HKUST (Hong Kong University of Science and Technology) series (Lee et al., 2020; Zhao et al., 2020). Their highly porous nature confers mixed matrix membranes with relatively large surface area for the adsorptive removal of contaminants from the environment as well as high selectivity since they have unique pore sizes. The incorporation of MOF fillers into the polysaccharide structures enables the designing of composite membranes with different pore sizes and functionalities that can be used for targeted applications. In addi­ tion, the composite membranes can withstand aggressive chemical en­ vironments since they have enhanced mechanical stabilities. Consequently, many researchers have directed their research efforts towards the development of mixed matrix membranes composed of polysaccharides and MOFs in recent years (Fazlifard et al., 2017; Kebria et al., 2019; Khajavian et al., 2020) (Fig. 1F).
There are many recent reviews on polysaccharide-based mixed ma­ trix membranes (Araujo et al., 2020; Choudhury et al., 2020; Douglass et al., 2018; Fan et al., 2020; Liu, Wu, et al., 2021; Mautner, 2020). For instance, Borgohain et al. (2021) reviewed the use of chitosan-based membranes for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture. Their review gave a detailed treatment of the structure-property relationship of chitosan membranes and the mechanisms involved during $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture. In the same vein, Spoiala˘ et al. (2021) published a paper on the utilisation of chitosan-based nanocomposite membranes in water treatment. Nakao et al. (2021), on the other hand, reviewed the application of cellulose hollow fibre membrane during desalination of seawater while Mbakop et al. (2021) wrote a detailed paper on the application of nanocellulose functional­ ised hybrid membranes during water treatment. None of these reviews were focussed on the incorporation of MOFs fillers into polysaccharide matrices to form versatile mixed matrix membranes with enhanced selectivity and mechanical strength. Thus, to the best of our knowledge, this paper is going to be among the first reviews to explore in detail the application of polysaccharide-based MOF membranes in separation science.
## 2. Application of polysaccharide-based MOF membranes in separation science
## 2.1. Chitosan-based MOF membranes
Chitosan is a polysaccharide obtained from a natural biopolymer called chitin, which is abundantly found in crustacean shells, exoskel­ eton of insects, fungi and cell walls (Roman-Hidalgo et al., 2021; Tang et al., 2021). Partial de-acetylation of chitin results in the formation of chitosan. Chitosan is among the biomaterials that have attracted the attention of many researchers during synthesis of membranes suitable for ultrafiltration (Ismail et al., 2021; Palacio et al., 2020), reverse osmosis (Kayani et al., 2021; Zakmout et al., 2020) and pervaporation (Achari et al., 2021; Banerjee & Ray, 2021). The attractive features of chitosan during membrane synthesis include its low cost, hydrophilicity and biodegradability. The use of pristine chitosan as membrane, how­ ever, has some limitations. Chitosan has low mechanical strength and it is highly brittle due to its high glass transition temperature (Borgohain et al., 2021; Gubanova et al., 2021; Pei et al., 2019). These limitations can be minimised through formation of composites with other polymers such as MOFs as well as chemical crosslinking (Table 1).
Many researchers have fabricated chitosan membrane using MOFs in recent years (Khajavian et al., 2020; Ma et al., 2017; Neufeld et al., 2017; Shi et al., 2021) (Fig. 2A). The common MOFs used for the fabrication of chitosan and other polysaccharides include HKUST-1 (Fig. 2B), MIL-101 (Cr) (Fig. 2C), $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) (Fig. 2D) and ZIF-8 (Fig. 2E). For instance, Ma et al. (2017) fabricated chitosan membrane with MIL-based MOFs. They synthesised two MOFs, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Al) and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ , which they homogeneously dispersed into chitosan polymeric matrix to form chitosan-MIL membranes. The mixed matrix membranes formed had stable structures. The stability of these composites mem­ branes can be attributed to complexation between the metal centres of the MIL MOFs with the amino and hydroxyl groups in chitosan. In addition, there were strong interactions between the aminofunctionalised MIL MOFs and chitosan through the formation of hydrogen bonds. The membrane was designed such that it contained basic groups (amino groups) that would become positively charged in acidic conditions thereby making it to be selectively permeable to cat­ ions. The permeability of these membranes to cationic and anionic ions were compared. Under acidic conditions, protonation of the $\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}$ -groups would occur making the composite membrane to be positively charged. Anionic ions would be adsorbed strongly and retained longer in the membrane due to strong electrostatic interactions between them and the protonated amino groups. Thus, the membranes were more permeable to cations. Structural characterisation of the membranes was done using XRD, FTIR and SEM. They found that the morphologies of the MIL MOFs had significant bearing on permeability of these membranes to cationic and anionic ions. The chitosan membrane filled with $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Al), with rod-like structure, attained two times higher permeation flux compared with that filled with $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ that had grainy structure.
In another study, Vinu et al. (2019) synthesised an Al-MOF, [Al(OH) (BPDC)] (BPDC $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ Biphenyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate) using solvothermal re­ actions and successfully incorporated them into the chitosan polymer to from a mixed matrix membrane, Al-MOF $@$ chitosan. Chelation between the ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ metal centres in the Al-MOF and the functionalities in chitosan conferred this mixed matrix membrane with remarkable stability. The stability of this composite membrane was further enhanced by the for­ mation of strong hydrogen bonds between carboxyl groups of the organic linkers and the functional groups (OH and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{,}$ in chitosan. The composite membrane they synthesised was hydrophilic and conse­ quently, it could facilitate the dehydration of ethanol. Thus, they incorporated hydrophilic groups in the membrane so as to enable it to have a high affinity of water and this facilitated the pervaporation process. In addition, the smaller size of water as compared to ethanol molecules also facilitated their preferential permeation. The hydrophilic nature of the membrane was enhanced by the presence of the negatively charged carboxylate groups from the organic ligands in the MOF fillers. They optimised the amount of the Al-MOF required as fillers during the pervaporation of water/ethanol at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using Al-MOF $@$ chitosan mixed matrix membrane. They observed that excellent permeability and selectivity in ethanol/water separation occurred the mixed matrix membrane contained $0.15~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of Al-MOF. The experimental results indicated that compared with pristine chitosan membranes, the flux of Al-MOF $@$ chitosan membranes with $0.15~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Al-MOF loading signifi­ cantly increased from 315 to $378\ \mathrm{g\m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ while the separation factor increased from 347 to 3429. As already alluded to, this can be attributed to the enhanced hydrophilicity of the composite membrane due to the MOF fillers. These promising results of the microporous Al-MOF doped into chitosan MMMs revealed its good application potential for the bioethanol separation processes.
Shi et al. (2021) fabricated polyethersulfone (PESU) membrane with tannic acid, chitosan and UiO-66 MOF to form a mixed matrix mem­ brane that they successfully used to remove both the anionic and cationic dye (acid brilliant scarlet and methylene blue) as well as Cr (VI) from water. PESU membrane consists of aromatic rings joined alterna­ tively by ether and sulfone groups. The ether and sulfone groups in the membrane confer it with polarity and hydrophilicity. The incorporation of chitosan into PESU structure enhanced its hydrophilicity and the UiO66 fillers improved its mechanical strength. The ether and sulfone groups are highly polar molecules. As a result, they formed ion-dipole interactions with the charged dyes and Cr (VI) ions making them to be retained in the membrane matrix. In addition, the separation of the charged dyes from water was made possible through the formation of $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions between the aromatic rings of the dyes and PESU. They optimised the parameters that affect the membrane performance such as pH, extraction time and amount of matrix fillers. They observed that the pollutant removal reached $99\%$ for $200~\mathrm{{min}}$ during filtration when the ratio of MOF/Chitosan was $30\%$ at pH of 3.5. The pollutant loading capacity in dynamic filtration was observed to be 3 times greater than in static adsorption. This can be attributed to the enhanced driving force brought by the concentration polarization in the filtration. Structural characterisation of the tannic acid/chitosan/MOF mixture was done using dynamic light scattering as well as Zeta potential, FTIR, XRD, TEM and XPS while the morphology of the membrane was studied using SEM and TEM. The structural elucidation result showed that the UiO-66 MOF facilitated the dispersion of the tannic acid-chitosan mixture throughout the PESU membrane and as a result its pollutant removal capacity was enhanced. On the other hand, TEM images showed the tannic acid/ chitosan mixture formed a uniform coating outside the MOF particle at $\mathrm{pH}3.5$ .
Casado-Coterillo et al. (2015) synthesised a mixed matrix mem­ branes by combining an ionic liquid, chitosan and either ZIF-8 or HKUST-1 MOF and used it for the separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ mixtures. The adhesive forces between the components of this composite membrane were largely due to chelation, hydrogen bonding and electrostatic in­ teractions. The presence of ZIF-8 or HKUST-1 MOF particles caused an improvement of the selectivity of the composite membrane since they have unique pore sizes as well as an increase in mechanical stability. The incorporation of an ionic liquid into the chitosan matrix introduced functionalities that enhanced the extraction efficiency of the membrane. They characterised the membrane using SEM, TEM and TGA. The TGA results showed that the presence of both ionic liquid and ZIF-8 or HKUST-1 fillers enhanced the thermal stability of the composite mem­ brane. They performed experiments to investigate the factors (amount of fillers, temperature and pH) that influence of the membrane. The single gas permeation performances for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ were investigated at temperatures in the range of $25{-}50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The optimum permeability of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ when $10\mathrm{\wt\%}$ ZIF-8 and $5\mathrm{wt\%}$ HKUST-1 were embedded into chitosan matrix were found to be $5413\pm191$ and $4754\pm1388$ Barrer, respec­ tively. On the other hand, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity performance under the same conditions were 11.5 ( $10~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ZIF-8) and 19.3 ( $5\mathrm{wt\%}$ HKUST-1). This can be attributed to better adhesion and smaller particle size of ZIF8 than HKUST-1 nanoparticles with respect to the ionic liquid-chitosan continuous matrix.
In another study, Khajavian et al. (2020) fabricated chitosan/poly­ vinyl alcohol composite membrane with ZIF-8 and successfully used it for adsorptive removal of malachite green dye from aqueous environment. The composite membrane they synthesised was relatively stable and hydrophilic. They designed a membrane that was hydrophilic so as to enable it to separate cationic malachite green dye from water through the formation of electrostatic interactions as well as $\pi{-}\pi$ in­ teractions between the aromatic rings of the malachite green dye and imidazolate ligands of ZIF-8. Chelation between the metal centres of ZIF8 and the functional groups in the chitosan/polyvinyl alcohol mixed matrix membrane could have contributed to the stability of the com­ posite. In addition, ion-dipole interactions between the imidazolate li­ gands of ZIF-8 and the OH and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups also conferred the composite with extra stability. Structural elucidation of the mixed matrix mem­ brane was done using SEM, scanning, dynamic light scattering, XRD, BET and FT-IR. The results of structural characterisation results showed that ZIF-8 fillers were embedded into the chitosan/polyvinyl alcohol composite membrane matrix. The roughness of the morphology of chitosan/ZIF-8 composites depends on the percentage of ZIF-8 used. Fig. 2F is the SEM image of pure chitosan, with a relatively smooth surface. Incorporation of different amounts of ZIF-8 into its structure caused significant changes in the roughness of its morphology (Fig. 2G and H). They optimised the parameters that affect the performance of the ZIF-8/chitosan/polyvinyl alcohol composite membrane. The optimal dye removal of $84.55\%$ was obtained when the membrane was doped with $1\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ ZIF-8. They then optimised the batch adsorption conditions such as mass of adsorbent, dye concentration and pH for the membrane containing $1\mathrm{\textrm{wt}\%}$ ZIF-8 using central composite experi­ mental design algorithm. The kinetics and equilibrium of the adsorption of malachite green dye from aqueous environment using ZIF-8/ chitosan/polyvinyl alcohol composite membrane could be satisfacto­ rily described using pseudo-second order model and the Langmuir isotherm. Experimental results showed that chitosan/polyvinyl alcohol composite was an effective membrane for dye separation from water.
Jiamjirangkul et al. (2020), on the other hand, successfully prepared mixed matrix membrane for the CO2 and N2 separation through the integration of Cu-BTC MOF into chitosan/polyvinyl alcohol nanofibrous membrane matrix. The inclusion of polyvinyl alcohol into the composite membrane conferred it with the following attractive properties: good mechanical properties, hydrophilicity, thermal and chemicals resis­ tance, anti-fouling potential, and film forming ability (Sapalidis, 2020). On the other hand, the highly porous nature of the Cu-BTC MOF greatly enhanced the surface area of the composite. They prepared Cu-BTCintegrated chitosan/PVA nanofibrous membrane (Cu-BTC/PVA/CNFs) hybrids using layer-by-layer depositing ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ as metal cluster and BTC as organic precursor on the surface chitosan nanofibers. The formation of the composite nanofibrous membrane was facilitated by the large sur­ face area of chitosan nanofibers as well as the presence of a large number of functional groups for cationic/anionic binding. In addition, there was also the possibility of complexation between the $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ metal centres with the functionalities in chitosan/polyvinyl alcohol composite membrane matrix resulting in increased stability. Their experimental results showed that the specific surface area and pore size distribution of the Cu-BTC/PVA/CNFs could be controlled by changing the order the pre­ cursors were mixed. They found out that by immersing chitosan/PVA nanofibrous membranes into the copper solution prior to the organic solution, there was an improvement in the specific surface area of CuBTC/PVA/CNFs as well as the development of micropores with a diameter in the range of $0.6\mathrm{-}0.8~\mathrm{nm}$ . Consequently, the adsorption ca­ pacity and selectivity of the Cu-BTC/PVA/CNFs membrane remarkably increased.
Pishnamazi et al. (2020) fabricated polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) nanofibrous membrane with chitosan and UiO-66- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ resulting in the formation of PVDF/chitosan/UiO-66- ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ composite. The presence of PVDF in the composite membrane improved its thermal stability and mechanical strength. In addition, PVDF has resistance to aggressive chemical environments such as acidic and basic conditions as well as organic solvents (Saxena & Shukla, 2021). Thus, these properties of PVDF combined with those from UiO-66 (such as large surface area)
resulted in the formation of a robust membrane with remarkable chemical and mechanical stabilities. During the synthetic process, they started by coating chitosan nanofibers with PVDF, followed by the incorporation of Ui ${\mathsf{O}}{\mathsf{-}}66{\mathsf{-N H}}_{2}$ MOF into the PVDF/chitosan matrix. Chelation between the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ metal centres of $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and chitosan functionalities as well as hydrogen bonding stabilised the composite membrane. The resultant nanofibrous membrane, PVDF/chitosan/UiO$66\small{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , was then used during the ultrafiltration of bovine serum albu­ min (BSA) protein and Cr (VI) from water samples. Proteins in water usually exist in their zwitterionic forms. The $\mathrm{cr}$ (VI) cations and the protein zwitterions formed strong electrostatic interactions with the composite membrane functionalities resulting in their adsorption on the membrane surface. The relatively small pore size $(0.6~\mathrm{nm})$ of UiO-66 conferred the composite membrane with some selectivity. Thus, sepa­ ration of BSA protein and $\mathrm{cr}$ (VI) from water can be explained in terms of size exclusion principles. Water molecules, with an average diameter of $0.275~\mathrm{nm}$ , could pass through the pores of the UiO-66 fillers in PVDF/ chitosan/UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ hydrophilic membrane. On the other hand, it was difficult for protein molecules to pass through the pores of UiO-66 due to their large size. Structural characterisation of the membrane was done using SEM, EDX, TGA, BET, porosity and water contact angle mea­ surements. The results from structural characterisation showed that $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ fillers were embedded into the chitosan matrix. They also optimised the parameters that affect the performance of the PVDF/ chitosan/UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ nanofibrous membrane. The composite nano­ fibrous membrane containing $20~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ , exhibited the maximum water flux of $470\mathrm{L}.\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , $98.1\%$ BSA rejection and $95.6\%$ Cr (VI) rejection. The nanofibers were sucessfully regenerated for five adsorption-desorption cycles.
## 2.2. Cellulose-based MOF membranes
Cellulose is one of the most abundant bio-polymer that is a promising nature-based adsorbent that can be used to synthesise membranes (Ashraf et al., 2021; Behrouz et al., 2021; Han et al., 2021). It consists of several D-glucose units linked by $^{1,4^{\prime}}$ - $\upbeta$ -glycoside bonds. Cellulose forms very strong intermolecular and intramolecular hydrogen bonds between the hydroxyl groups (Muhmed et al., 2021; Salidkul et al., 2021). These strong hydrogens bonds cause cellulose to aggregate into bundles and form crystalline structure. The aggregation of cellulose reduces the performance of pristine cellulose membranes. The incorporation of fillers such as MOFs into matrix of cellulose membranes greatly en­ hances their performance as well as their chemical and mechanical stabilities (Wang et al., 2021a; Yang et al., 2019). Despite these ad­ vantages, the use of cellulose-based MOF membranes is riddled with some limitations such as the occurrence of crack defects during synthesis (Table 2).
Many researcher have fabricated cellulose membranes with MOFs (Karmakar et al., 2017; Kim et al., 2019; Wang et al., 2021a; Yang et al., 2019) (Table 1) (Fig. 3A). For instance, Abdelhameed et al. (2021) synthesised a highly adsorptive porous membrane using cellulose ace­ tate (CA) fabricated with Cu-BTC MOF. The mixed matrix membrane was relatively stable. Chelation between the functionalities in cellulose acetate with ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ metal centres in Cu-BTC MOF had stabilising effects on the composite membrane. In addition, hydrogen bonds formed be­ tween the carboxyl groups in the BTC ligands and the OH groups in cellulose could have contributed to the stability of the mixed matrix membrane. They then used the mixed matrix membrane, Cu-BTC@CA, for the adsorptive removal of dimethoate from water samples. The porous $\mathtt{C u-B T C}@\mathtt{C A}$ membrane was synthesised in two steps. They initially synthesised the CA membrane and this was followed by the in situ synthesis of Cu-BTC on its surface. The parameters that affect the performance of the Cu-BTC@CA membrane were optimised. The effect of the amount of MOF incorporated into the membrane was investigated by varying the ratio Cu-BTC from 20 to $60\%$ . They observed that maximum performance of Cu-BTC@CA membrane was obtained when
$40\%$ of Cu-BTC was incorporated into the Cu-BTC@CA membrane. Structural elucidation of the membrane was done sing FT-IR, SEM and BET. The characterisation results showed that Cu-BTC was successfully immobilised in the CA membrane with a pore diameter of 112.6–496.0 nm. The BET results, on the other hand, revealed the remarkable in­ crease in surface area from $78.4\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for CA membrane to $965.8\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $40\%$ Cu-BTC@CA membrane. Consequently, the incorporation of CuBTC caused an improvement in adsorption capacity from $207.8\ \mathrm{mg\g^{-1}}$ for CA membrane to $282.3\substack{-321.9\mathrm{~mg~g}^{-1}}$ for the Cu-BTC@CA mem­ brane. The membrane was reusable several times without significant loss in permeability and selectivity.
The physico-chemical properties of cellulose membranes can be improved by imbedding ZIF MOF fillers in their structures. For instance, Zhang et al. (2018) fabricated carboxymethyl cellulose with ZIF nano­ sheets (ZIF-L) to form an effective mixed matrix composite membrane for gas separation (Fig. 3B). The incorporation of ZIF-L nanosheets with a pore size of $3.8\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ into the matrix of carboxymethyl cellulose conferred the composite membrane with molecular sieving properties. Carbox­ ymethyl cellulose, on the other hand, forms a gas barrier layer in the composite membrane. They prepared the nanosheets using zinc salts and 2-methylimidazole prior to their incorporation into a carboxymethyl cellulose solution to form a steady mixed aqueous suspension through one-step solution blending. This resulted in the formation of a composite membrane with a fine dispersion of ZIF-L nanosheets and good adhesion within the carboxymethyl cellulose matrix (Table 2). The good adhesion among the polymeric units of the membrane can be attributed to the strong interactions between ZIF-L and carboxymethyl cellulose. The performed structural characterisation experiments using FTIR, Zeta potential, XPS, and SEM. The results of structural characterisation ex­ periments confirmed the strong integration between ZIF-L nanosheets and carboxymethyl cellulose matrix in the membrane. This mixed ma­ trix membrane had better gas selectivity compared with the carbox­ ymethyl cellulose membrane and pure ZIF membranes. This can be attributed to the combined effects of the molecular sieving properties of ZIF-L and the gas barrier nature of the carboxymethyl cellulose matrix.
In another study, Song et al. (2019) synthesised cellulose nano­ fibrous composite membranes by in-situ growing of ZIF-8 MOF as spacers in the presence of TEMPO oxidized cellulose nanofibers (TOCN) as their anchoring points. This resulted in the formation a porous membrane composed of a three-dimensional network TOCN and ZIF-8. The networked structure gave the composite membrane high dura­ bility and mechanical strength without critical compaction of the membrane under pressure. Experimental results showed that the $20\mathrm{{mu}}$ m thick ZIF-8/TOCN membrane had the optimum water flux of $84\mathrm{Lm}^{-2}$ $\mathrm{\h}{-}1\ \mathrm{bar}^{-1}$ without critical flux drop for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ operation. The composite membrane was used for the separation of cationic and anionic dyes. They observed that the mixed matrix membrane could selectively remove cationic dyes in the presence of anionic dyes. This can be attributed to the strong electrostatic interaction the cationic dyes and the negatively charged TOCN networks.
Mubashir et al. (2021), on the other hand, successfully synthesised cellulose acetate (CA) membranes fabricated with ZIF-62 MOF glass to form a mixed matrix membrane (ZIF-62/CA), which they used for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gas separation. Gas permeation experimental results, using and mixed gases, showed that the presence of ZIF-62 glass in CA polymer enhanced the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeability from 15.8 Barrer in CA membranes to 84.8 Barrer in ZIF-62/CA composite. In addition, the fabrication of CA matrix with ZIF-62 glass improved the $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ selectivity from 15.8 to 84.8. Experimental results also revealed that the ZIF-62/CA composite membrane loaded with $8\mathrm{\mt{\%}}$ of ZIF-62 glass had the highest $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeability and $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ ideal selectivity of 84.8 Barrer and 35.3, which were $436.7\%$ and $189.3\%$ higher than those of pristine CA membrane, respectively. The incorporation of ZIF-62 glass into the CA matrix also enhanced the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ plasticization pressure of the membrane. The $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ plasticization pressure of the ZIF-62/CA composite membrane was found to be 26 bars and this was $160\%$ higher than that of pristine
CA membrane which is approximately 10 bars. Thus, ZIF-62 glass is a promising matrix filler during the synthesis of MOF-fabricated cellulose composite membranes.
The chemical and mechanical stability of cellulose membranes can be enhanced also by the incorporation of UiO MOF into their matrices. UiO MOFs have remarkable stability in water and have relatively high mechanical strength. El-Mehalmey et al. (2020), for instance, syn­ thesised a durable mixed matrix membrane by the integration of UiO$66\substack{-\Delta\mathrm{VH}_{2}}$ MOFs into the CA matrix. Complexation between the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ metal centres and the hydroxyl groups in cellulose conferred the composite membrane with stability. In addition, hydrogen bonds formation be­ tween the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups in UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ MOFs and the OH groups in cel­ lulose provided extra stability to the mixed matrix membrane. The UiO$66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{CA}$ composite membrane they synthesised had superior per­ formance in terms of exceptional removal of both cationic and anionic dyes as well as hexavalent Cr ions from wastewater as compared to pristine CA membrane. The filtration of cationic species through the membrane was facilitated by the presence of negative charges on the surface of the matrix. These negative surface charges, however, made it difficult for anionic species to diffuse and reach the active UiO- $66\substack{-\Delta\mathrm{VH}_{2}}$ fillers. Moreover, UiO-66- ${\mathrm{.NH}}_{2}/{\mathrm{CA}}$ composite membrane demonstrated high durability and recyclability with no significant loss of performance over numerous cycles.
## 2.3. Cyclodextrin-based MOF membranes
Cyclodextrin-based membranes have become common materials in separation science due to their high porosities, small fibre diameters and high surface area to volume ratio (Lu et al., 2021a; Topuz et al., 2021). In addition, cyclodextrins are environmentally friendly and have unique molecular structures that enable them to form unique interactions with various pollutants. These interactions include inclusion complexation, hydrophobic interaction, weak van der Waals forces and charge transfer interactions (Khalil et al., 2021; Lagiewka et al., 2021). $\upbeta$ -cyclodextrin is soluble in water in its pure form (Lim et al., 2021; Lorenzo-Veiga et al., 2021). It is, however, rendered insoluble by crosslinking with materials such as citric acid as well as formation of composites with polymeric materials such as PAN and MOFs (Dodero et al., 2021; Liang et al., 2021). The use of cyclodextrin (CD)-based membrane fabricated with MOFs is slowly gaining momentum in separation science (Fig. 4A), especially during chiral resolution (Table 2).
Some researchers have fabricated CD-based membranes with MOFs (Lu et al., 2021b; Mehmood et al., 2021) (Table 1). For instance, Lu et al. (2021a, 2021b) synthesised an enantioselective mixed matrix mem­ brane using homochiral MOFs, CD and polyethersulfone (PES). During the synthesis, they used CD-MOF as a chiral selector and PES as the matrix resulting in the formation of a composite membrane with stable enantioselectivity. They used the enantioselective membrane for the resolution of the enantiomers of phenylethanol. Experiments to inves­ tigate parameters that affect the performance of the membrane were conducted. The experimental results revealed that the CD-MOF/PES mixed matrix membrane with a $20~\mathrm{wt\%}$ CD-MOF loading achieved a stable and nearly $100\%$ enantioselectivity of R- $(+)$ -1-phenylethanol over S-( )-1-phenylethanol when non-polar n-hexane is used as the solvent (Fig. 4B). In addition, it was observed that the CD-MOF/PES membrane's enantioselectivity decreased with the permeation time when polar methanol and ethanol were used as solvents. Thus, the interaction of solvent with chiral molecules and MOF structure is one of the important factors to be considered during the synthesis and design of enantioselective CD-MOF-based membranes.
Mehmood et al. (2020), on the other hand, fabricated polyurethanebased composite membranes with MOFs and used them for $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ separation. During the synthesis, they used CD-MOFs as fillers and polyurethane membrane as matrix. The fabrication of the composite membrane with polyurethane conferred it substantial physical and tensile strength, good mechanical strength and high chemical resistance. They characterised the mixed matrix membrane using FTIR, SEM, XRD and UTS. The structural elucidation results showed that CD-MOF, with an ideal cavity of $17\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ diameter and an abundance of the hydroxyl group, were imbedded into the polyurethane membrane matrix. These unique characteristics of the composite membrane facilitated the adsorption of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ from the $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ gas mixture and its subsequent diffusion through the pores of the membrane. The adsorption of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ can be attributed to the formation of dipole-dipole interactions between it and the OH groups in the membrane. The membrane performance was tested using single and mixed gas permeation tests. The experimental results showed that fabrication of polyurethane membrane with CDMOF caused the enhancement of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeability. The best $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ selectivity of 28.56 was obtained when polyurethane membrane loaded with $0.2~\mathrm{wt}\%$ of CD-MOF was used.
In another study, Mehmood et al. (2021) synthesised a defect-free CD-MOF mixed matrix membrane with dense, isotropic morphology using solution casting method. The membrane, composed of CA, CD and MOFs, was used for $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ separation. The adhesive forces between the components of the mixed matrix membrane were mainly due to chelation and the formation of hydrogen bonds. These interactions conferred the membrane with relatively high stability. They used FT-IR, XRD, SEM, ultimate tensile testing, and contact angle measurements for the structural characterisation of CA/CD-MOF mixed matrix membrane. The characterisation results showed that the CA/CD-MOF mixed matrix membrane had uniform dispersion of filler in cellulose acetate matrix and had no agglomerates or cracks on its surface. The absence of cracks enhanced the efficiency of the composite membrane due the absence of gas leakages during separation. The performance of the mixed matrix membrane was evaluated using single and mixed gas permeation tests. They observed a general decrease in permeability and selectivity of both $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ when the MOF filler weight percentage $(0.2\mathrm{-}1.0\%)$ ) and pressure (1–5 bar) were increased. The highest $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ selectivity of 38.49 was obtained when Ca/CD-MOF composite membrane with 0.4 wt $\%$ of MOF was used.
## 2.4. Alginate-based MOF membranes
Alginates are linear anionic polysaccharides that are derived from kelp or Sargassum algae of brown algae as well as from several bacterial strains (Herna´ndez-Gonza´lez et al., 2020; Zhang et al., 2021a). They are copolymers mainly composed of $\upbeta$ -D-mannuronic acid (M) and α-Lguluronic acid (G) residues that are linked together via $^{1,4}$ -glycosidic bond in an irregular block-wise manner. The M and G residues are organized in homopolymeric blocks of G units (GG blocks) or M units (MM blocks) and heteropolymeric sequences of randomly coupled G and M units (GM or MG blocks) (Uyen et al., 2020; Zhang et al., 2020; Zhang et al., 2021a) (Fig. 4A). Alginate have been used by some researchers during the formation of membranes. Its use in membranes, however, is hampered by poor chemical and mechanical stability under aggressive environmental conditions. Its chemical and mechanical stability can be greatly enhanced by the formation of composites with stable materials such as MOFs (Castro-Mun˜oz & Gonz´alez-Valdez, 2019).
Some researchers have fabricated alginate with MOFs such as MILs (Su et al., 2015) and ZIFs (Li et al., 2019) during formation of mixed matrix membranes (Table 1) (Fig. 5A). For instance, Su et al. (2015) synthesised amine-functionalized metal organic framework $\mathrm{(NH_{2}}$ -MIL125(Ti)) crystal nanoparticles and incorporated them into sodium algi­ nate (NaAlg) matrix resulting in the formation of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)/ NaAlg mixed matrix membrane. Adhesion of the components of the membrane was sustained by complexation between the metal centres of the MIL MOF and the functional groups on alginate. In addition, in­ teractions through formation of hydrogen bonds between the amino groups of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) MOF and the hydroxyl groups of alginate helped to keep the composite membrane stable. The membrane, after its synthesis, was used during the pervaporation of water-acetic acid mixture. Structural elucidation of the synthesised $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) crystal nanoparticles and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)/NaAlg mixed matrix mem­ branes was done using FT-IR, XRD, AFM, TEM and contact angle goniometer. The characterisation results showed that the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti)/NaAlg mixed matrix membranes had functionalities that would enable selective absorption of water molecules from the water-acetic acid mixture as well as rough morphology that ensured the presence of a large surface area during the separation process. The pervaporation performance of the prepared $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)/NaAlg mixed matrix membrane was tested and compared with a pristine NaAlg membrane during the dehydration of acetic acid. The experimental results indi­ cated that $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)/NaAlg mixed matrix membrane had better separation performance than the pristine NaAlg membrane. This can be attributed to the presence of additional functionalities that facilitated the separation process. Thus, the incorporation of the $\mathrm{{NH}_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) nanoparticles into the structure of alginate resulted in the enhancement of its pervaporation performance during the dehydration of acetic acid.
In another study, Kuzminova et al. (2021a, 2021b) synthesised green membranes based on sodium alginate (SA) fabricated by Fe-BTC met­ al–organic framework and successfully used it during the pervaporation of water-isopropanol mixture. Chelation and intermolecular forces hel­ ped to keep the components of this mixed matrix membrane intact. They developed two kinds of SA-Fe-BTC mixed matrix membranes, the un­ treated membranes and the cross-linked membranes with citric acid or phosphoric acid as well as different percentages of Fe-BTC MOF (Fig. 5B). The performance of the crosslinked composite membranes was better as compared to the untreated membranes. This can be attributed to the fact that crosslinking conferred the membranes with enhanced mechanical strength. Structural characterisation of the synthesised membranes was done using FTIR, NMR, SEM, atomic force microscopy (AFM), TGA and swelling experiments. The AFM results showed that the roughness of the morphology of composite membrane was dependent on the percentage of Fe-BTC MOF in it (Fig. 5B). They studied the transport properties of developed alginate-based mixed matrix membranes in the pervaporation of water–isopropanol mixtures. The experimental results showed that pervaporation performance was optimum when $15\mathrm{wt\%}$ FeBTC was added to the alginate with citric acid as the cross-linker. The permeation flux of the membrane was found to be $\ 174{-}1584\ \mathrm{g\m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ and the water content in the permeate was relatively high $(99.99\mathrm{wt\%})$ . This was an indication of the high efficiency of the alginate – Fe-BTC mixed matrix membrane during the pervaporation of water­ –isopropanol mixtures.
## 3. Challenges and future prospects
Natural polysaccharide biopolymers have been used widely during remediation of the environment using membrane technologies (Machodi & Daramola, 2019; Mehmood et al., 2021; Rupiasih et al., 2016). Their direct use, however, is hampered by the fact that most of them are not stable under aggressive environmental conditions. As a result, mixed matrix membranes composed of polysaccharides and other materials such as MOFs are usually used during environmental remediation. The inclusion of MOFs in the polysaccharide structures results in the syn­ thesis of membranes with multifaceted networks and functionalities. In addition, composite membranes with tremendous chemical/thermal/ hydrothermal stabilities are formed that can withstand harsh chemical environments and thermally challenging conditions under real indus­ trial conditions.
The use of polysaccharide-based MOF membranes during the adsorptive removal of environmental pollutants is not free of challenges. The synthetic procedures of most MOFs usually involves the use of high temperatures and organic solvents. For instance, Ghaffar et al. (2019) reacted $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and terephthalic acid in dimethylformanide at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ during the synthesis of MIL-53 prior to its coating with chitosan. The use of elevated temperatures may cause secondary pollution of the environment by toxic by-products as well as by organic solvents. Thus, the synthetic routes for most polysaccharide/MOF composites are not appealing from an environmental point of view. The separation of pollutants using polysaccharide-based MOF membranes is sometimes based on size exclusion selectivity. Hendon et al. (2017), however, pointed out that size exclusion is difficult to employ during the separation of molecules with similar sizes and polarizability, as this would involve the tedious fine-tuning of the pore sizes of the composite membrane.
The MOFs to be used for the fabrication of polysaccharide mem­ branes should be carefully selected since not all of them are stable under aggressive conditions. For instance, MOFs materials that have divalent $\scriptstyle2\ n^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ nodes are unstable in water and aggressive conditions (Ban et al., 2020). Also, the organic ligands in MOFs have a great bearing in their stabilities. For instance, MOFs such as MOF-5 that contain bidentate carboxylate ligands are not stable under harsh chemical con­ ditions. Consequently, MOFs such as ZIFs, MILs and UiOs, with remarkable chemical and thermal stabilities, should be used for the fabrication of polysaccharide-based membranes. The stability of poly­ saccharide/MOF composite membranes can be enhanced by using MOFs with high valent metal nodes such as ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ as this would result in increase in the metal-linker bond strength. In addition, the use of metal nodes that are chemically inert can improve the chemical stability of polysaccharide/MOF composite membranes as this would minimise oxidative reactions during their use. Some polysaccharide/MOF com­ posite membranes undergo hydrolysis under aqueous conditions. These hydrolytic reactions can be reduced by the fabrication of the poly­ saccharide membranes with MOFs that have hydrophobic linkers (Ban et al., 2020, p. 202).
The nanoparticles used during the formation of polysaccharide/MOF nanocomposites can get into living organisms where they may cause damage at cellular or protein level due to their small size and large specific surface area. In addition, the nanoparticles interact with the immune system as well as biological macromolecules to form biological crowns (Zhang, 2020). As a result, the immune system would be severely compromised and the proper functioning of biological macromolecules such as proteins would be tremendously impaired. Thus, health-related issues should be considered seriously when using polysaccharide/MOF nanocomposite membranes in separation science.
Another challenge when using polysaccharide/MOF composite membranes is fouling. They can undergo organic, inorganic and bio­ logical fouling (Choudhury et al., 2020; Gafri et al., 2019). Membrane fouling has adverse effects on the quality of the permeate and results in flux decline during the separation process (Meng et al., 2019). Surface modification of the polysaccharide/MOF composite membranes can be employed to minimise fouling (Huang et al., 2018; Seo et al., 2018). This can be done through hybridization (Zhao et al., 2019), formation of a ultra-thin film (He et al., 2018) and brush-like layer (Wang et al., 2019) on the surface of the composite membrane (Fig. 6). For instance, Lav­ anya et al. (2021) successfully enhanced fouling resistance in polymeric membranes by formation of hybrid composites with oxygen-rich yttrium titanate. On the other hand, Kang et al. (2012) improved the fouling resistance of polyamide membrane by the attachment of poly(ethylene glycol) chains on its surface resulting in the formation of a brush-like layer on it. Application of similar strategies on polysaccharide/MOF composite membranes may also enhance their anti-fouling properties. In addition, the anti-fouling properties of the polysaccharide/MOF com­ posite membranes can be enhanced by covering their surface with an ultra-thin film in a bid to weaken the interactive forces between them and the foulants (Liu et al., 2017). The use of these anti-fouling strate­ gies, however, entail additional cost during membrane separation processes.
## 4. Conclusion
Polysaccharide-based membranes fabricated with MOFs are rela­ tively efficient separation techniques for a variety of organic and inor­ ganic pollutants. The incorporation of MOF fillers in the polysaccharide membrane matrices confer them with enhanced chemical and mechan­ ical stabilities as well as increased selectivity during the separation process. Enhanced selectivity of mixed matrix membrane fabricated with MOFs is usually attributed to their unique pore sizes, which allow selectivity based on size exclusion principles. Strong interactive forces are required to keep the components of these mixed matrix membranes intact. Complexation and intermolecular forces such as hydrogen bonds, $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions and dipole-dipole forces are usually involved in stabil­ ising these membranes.
